// Economics lessons — loaded on demand by ensureLessons('economics') in index.html.
// Shape: LESSON_DB.economics.m<moduleIndex>[topicIndex] = lesson. Order matches COURSE_DATA.economics.
// National statistics are rounded and dated — check Stats SA and the SARB for current figures.
LESSON_DB.economics = LESSON_DB.economics || {};

// ─── Module 0: Basic Concepts ───────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.economics.m0 = {
  0: {emoji:"⚖️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Unlimited wants, limited money",
    illusText:"R500, two choices: sneakers or a month of data. Whatever you give up is the real cost.",
    hook:{title:"R500 birthday money", body:"Thando gets R500 for her birthday. She wants new sneakers (R500) and a month of data (R500). She can't have both. If she chooses the sneakers, the <strong>real cost</strong> isn't just R500 — it's the month of data she gave up. Economists call that the <strong>opportunity cost</strong>. Governments, businesses and families face the same choice every day."},
    intro:"<strong>Economics</strong> studies how people use <strong>scarce resources</strong> to satisfy <strong>unlimited wants</strong>. Because resources are scarce, we must <strong>choose</strong>. The <strong>opportunity cost</strong> of a choice is the value of the <strong>next best alternative</strong> given up. A <strong>production possibility curve (PPC)</strong> shows the combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources: points on the curve are efficient, inside the curve show unused resources (e.g. unemployment), and points outside are unattainable for now.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔍 Scarcity", body:"Not enough resources (money, time, land, workers) to satisfy every want. Scarcity forces choice."},
      {t:"teal", title:"↩️ Opportunity cost", body:"Only the next best alternative — not every option you didn't choose."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📉 The PPC", body:"Moving along the curve shows the trade-off: more of one good means less of the other."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Personal choice", body:"Thando buys sneakers instead of data.", answer:"Opportunity cost = <strong>the month of data</strong> she gave up."},
      {title:"Government choice", body:"The government spends R10 billion on a new highway instead of new clinics.", answer:"Opportunity cost = <strong>the clinics</strong> that could have been built."},
      {title:"PPC", body:"An economy is producing at a point inside its PPC.", answer:"Resources are <strong>unused or wasted</strong> — e.g. high unemployment."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Listing every alternative as the opportunity cost — only the next best one counts.","Thinking scarcity only affects poor people.","Saying a point outside the PPC is inefficient — it's unattainable with current resources."],
    callout:{title:"Your time is scarce too", body:"An hour on TikTok has an opportunity cost — an hour of studying, sleeping or working. Economics is about every choice you make."},
    activity:"List three choices you made this week. For each, state the opportunity cost.",
    activityAnswer:"Example: watching a match (cost: an hour of revision); buying lunch (cost: saving the R40); taking a taxi (cost: the R15 and the exercise of walking). Answers will vary.",
    examTip:"Define opportunity cost precisely: “the value of the next best alternative forgone”. Then give an example.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Scarcity",d:"Limited resources compared with unlimited wants"},{w:"Opportunity cost",d:"The next best alternative given up"},{w:"PPC",d:"Curve showing the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Opportunity cost is:",opts:["The price paid","The next best alternative given up","All alternatives given up","A tax"],a:1,et:"Only the next best."},
      {q:"A point inside the PPC shows:",opts:["Efficient production","Unused resources","Impossible production","Economic growth"],a:1,et:"For example, unemployment."},
      {q:"The basic economic problem is:",opts:["Inflation","Scarcity","Exports","Taxes"],a:1,et:"Unlimited wants, limited resources."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Every choice has a cost — the next best thing you give up. That idea runs through all of Economics."},

  1: {emoji:"🍅", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Why prices go up and down",
    illusText:"After floods destroy tomato crops, the price at the market jumps within days.",
    hook:{title:"Why are tomatoes so expensive this week?", body:"After heavy rain and hail damage tomato farms in Limpopo, prices at the Joburg Market jump within days. Shoppers buy fewer tomatoes and switch to other vegetables. Nobody ordered prices to change — <strong>supply and demand</strong> did it. Understanding these forces explains everything from taxi fares to concert tickets."},
    intro:"<strong>Demand</strong> is the quantity buyers are willing and able to buy at different prices. The <strong>law of demand</strong>: as price rises, quantity demanded falls (and vice versa). <strong>Supply</strong> is the quantity sellers are willing to offer; the <strong>law of supply</strong>: as price rises, quantity supplied rises. A change in the good's own price causes a <strong>movement along</strong> the curve. A change in another factor causes a <strong>shift</strong> of the whole curve: demand shifts with income, tastes, prices of substitutes and complements, and population; supply shifts with input costs, technology, weather, taxes and the number of sellers.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📉 Demand", body:"Downward-sloping: higher price → fewer bought. Shifts right if incomes rise (normal goods) or a substitute gets dearer."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📈 Supply", body:"Upward-sloping: higher price → more offered. Shifts left after floods, droughts or higher fuel and input costs."},
      {t:"gold", title:"↔️ Movement vs shift", body:"Own price changes → move along the curve. Any other factor → the whole curve shifts."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Supply shock", body:"Hail destroys tomato crops.", answer:"<strong>Supply shifts left</strong> → higher price, smaller quantity."},
      {title:"Substitutes", body:"The price of chicken rises sharply.", answer:"<strong>Demand for substitutes</strong> (e.g. beans, eggs) shifts right."},
      {title:"Movement or shift?", body:"Taxi fares rise and commuters take fewer trips.", answer:"A <strong>movement along the demand curve</strong> — the fare (price) itself changed."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying “demand fell” when only quantity demanded fell because of a price change.","Shifting the wrong curve (weather affects supply, not demand).","Drawing supply sloping downwards."],
    callout:{title:"Petrol and everything else", body:"When fuel prices rise, transport costs rise for almost every product — supply curves across the economy shift left."},
    activity:"For each event, say which curve shifts and in which direction: (a) a new bakery opens in town; (b) a school starts selling cheaper vetkoek; (c) wages rise across the country.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) Supply of bread shifts right. (b) Demand for other vetkoek sellers' products shifts left (cheaper substitute). (c) Demand for normal goods shifts right.",
    examTip:"Always draw and label graphs (P and Q axes, D and S, original and new curves, arrows) — graphs earn marks on their own.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Law of demand",d:"Higher price → lower quantity demanded"},{w:"Law of supply",d:"Higher price → higher quantity supplied"},{w:"Substitute",d:"A good that can be used instead of another"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A drought affecting maize causes the supply of maize to:",opts:["Shift right","Shift left","Stay the same","Become free"],a:1,et:"Less can be produced."},
      {q:"If the price of a good rises, quantity demanded:",opts:["Rises","Falls","Stays the same","Doubles"],a:1,et:"Law of demand."},
      {q:"A rise in income usually shifts demand for normal goods to the:",opts:["Left","Right","It doesn't shift","Vertical"],a:1,et:"People can afford more."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Prices are signals. Supply and demand explain why they change — and how buyers and sellers respond."},

  2: {emoji:"🎟️", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Where supply meets demand",
    illusText:"A sold-out amapiano concert: tickets resold online for double the price signal a shortage.",
    hook:{title:"Sold out in 10 minutes", body:"A popular amapiano artist announces a stadium show. Tickets at R450 sell out in ten minutes, and soon they appear online for R900. Why? At R450, far more people want tickets than there are seats — a <strong>shortage</strong>. Markets push towards the price where the quantity people want equals the quantity available: <strong>equilibrium</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Market equilibrium</strong> is where <strong>quantity demanded = quantity supplied</strong>. The equilibrium price clears the market. If the price is <strong>above</strong> equilibrium, there's a <strong>surplus</strong> (excess supply) and sellers cut prices. If it's <strong>below</strong>, there's a <strong>shortage</strong> (excess demand) and prices rise. Governments sometimes set a <strong>maximum price (price ceiling)</strong>, which can cause shortages, or a <strong>minimum price (price floor)</strong> — the national minimum wage is a floor in the labour market. With equations: set Qd = Qs and solve for P.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎯 Equilibrium", body:"The price where buyers and sellers agree on quantity — no pressure to change."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📦 Surplus", body:"Price too high → unsold stock → sellers lower prices (think year-end sales)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏃 Shortage", body:"Price too low → queues, sell-outs and resale markets → upward pressure on price."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Solve it", body:"Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P.", answer:"100 − 2P = 20 + 2P → 4P = 80 → <strong>P = R20</strong>; Q = 100 − 40 = <strong>60 units</strong>."},
      {title:"Shortage", body:"At P = R15 in the same market, what happens?", answer:"Qd = 70, Qs = 50 → <strong>shortage of 20 units</strong>; price will rise."},
      {title:"Price floor", body:"Why is the minimum wage a price floor?", answer:"It sets the <strong>lowest legal price for labour</strong>; if set above equilibrium, it can create a surplus of labour (unemployment) — economists debate how large this effect is."},
    ],
    formula:"Equilibrium: Quantity demanded = Quantity supplied",
    mistakes:["Mixing up surplus (price too high) and shortage (price too low).","Forgetting to calculate Q after finding P.","Drawing a ceiling above equilibrium and saying it causes a shortage — it must be below to bind."],
    callout:{title:"Load shedding and generators", body:"When load shedding hit hard, demand for generators and inverters jumped, causing shortages and higher prices until supply caught up."},
    activity:"Qd = 120 − 3P and Qs = 30 + 3P. Find the equilibrium price and quantity.",
    activityAnswer:"120 − 3P = 30 + 3P → 6P = 90 → <strong>P = 15</strong>; Q = 120 − 45 = <strong>75</strong>.",
    examTip:"Label equilibrium as Pe and Qe on your graph and show the surplus or shortage as a horizontal gap.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Equilibrium",d:"Where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied"},{w:"Shortage",d:"Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied"},{w:"Price floor",d:"A legal minimum price"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"If the price is above equilibrium there is a:",opts:["Shortage","Surplus","Equilibrium","Recession"],a:1,et:"Excess supply."},
      {q:"Qd = 60 − P, Qs = 2P. Equilibrium price:",opts:["R15","R20","R30","R40"],a:1,et:"60 − P = 2P → P = 20."},
      {q:"The national minimum wage is an example of a:",opts:["Price ceiling","Price floor","Subsidy","Tax"],a:1,et:"A legal minimum price for labour."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Markets move towards equilibrium. Surpluses push prices down, shortages push them up."},

  3: {emoji:"🧱", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship",
    illusText:"Even a kota shop needs all four factors of production.",
    hook:{title:"What it takes to sell a kota", body:"To sell one kota, Bongani needs a spot to trade from (<strong>land</strong>), someone to cook and serve (<strong>labour</strong>), a stove, fridge and utensils (<strong>capital</strong>), and the idea, the courage and the risk of starting the business (<strong>entrepreneurship</strong>). Every product in the economy — from a kota to a Hilux — uses these four <strong>factors of production</strong>."},
    intro:"The four <strong>factors of production</strong> and their rewards: <strong>Land</strong> (natural resources — soil, minerals, water) earns <strong>rent</strong>. <strong>Labour</strong> (human effort, physical and mental) earns <strong>wages</strong> and salaries. <strong>Capital</strong> (man-made goods used to produce other goods — machines, tools, buildings) earns <strong>interest</strong>. <strong>Entrepreneurship</strong> (organising the other factors and taking risks) earns <strong>profit</strong>. In Economics, “capital” means equipment and tools, not money itself. South Africa is rich in land (minerals) and labour, but needs more capital and skills.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⛏️ Land", body:"All natural resources — including platinum, coal, farmland, sea and sunshine."},
      {t:"teal", title:"👩‍🔧 Labour and skills", body:"The quality of labour (human capital) depends on education, training and health."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🚀 Entrepreneurship", body:"Combines the other factors, carries risk and earns profit — or makes a loss."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Classify", body:"A taxi owner's minibus.", answer:"<strong>Capital</strong> — a man-made good used to provide a service."},
      {title:"Reward", body:"What does a landowner earn for renting out a field?", answer:"<strong>Rent</strong>."},
      {title:"Not money", body:"Is R50 000 in the bank “capital” in Economics?", answer:"<strong>No</strong> — it's financial capital; in Economics, capital means goods used in production (e.g. machines)."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Calling money a factor of production.","Mixing up the rewards (land → rent, labour → wages, capital → interest, entrepreneur → profit).","Forgetting that land includes all natural resources, not just ground."],
    callout:{title:"Human capital", body:"Every year of quality schooling raises a person's productivity and earning power — investing in yourself is investing in a factor of production."},
    activity:"Choose a local business. List one example of each factor of production it uses and the reward each earns.",
    activityAnswer:"Example for a hair salon: land — the premises (rent); labour — stylists (wages); capital — chairs, dryers (interest); entrepreneurship — the owner (profit).",
    examTip:"Learn the factors as a table with their rewards — it's an easy 4–8 marks.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Factors of production",d:"Land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship"},{w:"Capital",d:"Man-made goods used to produce other goods"},{w:"Entrepreneur",d:"Person who organises production and takes risks"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The reward for labour is:",opts:["Rent","Wages","Interest","Profit"],a:1,et:"Wages and salaries."},
      {q:"A sewing machine in a clothing factory is:",opts:["Land","Labour","Capital","Entrepreneurship"],a:2,et:"Man-made, used in production."},
      {q:"Platinum in the ground is classified as:",opts:["Land","Labour","Capital","Profit"],a:0,et:"A natural resource."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship — every product needs all four, and each earns its reward."},

  4: {emoji:"🏛️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Who decides what gets produced?",
    illusText:"Market, command or mixed — South Africa combines private business with government action.",
    hook:{title:"Who decides what's on the shelf?", body:"Why does your local shop stock 20 kinds of chips but the clinic sometimes runs out of medicine? Different parts of the economy are run in different ways. In some countries, the government decides almost everything; in others, businesses and customers do. South Africa uses a mix. Every country must answer three questions: <strong>what</strong> to produce, <strong>how</strong> and <strong>for whom</strong>."},
    intro:"An <strong>economic system</strong> is how a society answers the three basic questions. In a <strong>market (capitalist) economy</strong>, private individuals own resources and prices guide decisions — efficient and innovative, but it can increase inequality and under-provide public goods. In a <strong>command (centrally planned) economy</strong>, the state owns resources and plans production — it can target equality, but often suffers from shortages and inefficiency (e.g. the former Soviet Union, North Korea). A <strong>mixed economy</strong> combines both: private businesses operate in markets, while government provides public goods (roads, defence), regulates, taxes and redistributes. <strong>South Africa has a mixed economy.</strong>",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🏪 Market", body:"Prices, profit and competition decide what's produced. Consumers “vote” with their money."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📋 Command", body:"Government plans what, how and for whom. Prices are set by the state."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🇿🇦 Mixed", body:"In South Africa: private companies plus state-owned enterprises (Eskom, Transnet), social grants and regulation."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Identify the system", body:"The government decides how many shoes each factory must produce and sets their price.", answer:"<strong>Command economy</strong>."},
      {title:"SA as mixed", body:"Give two examples showing South Africa is a mixed economy.", answer:"<strong>Private businesses</strong> like supermarkets and banks; <strong>state involvement</strong> like Eskom, social grants and public schools."},
      {title:"Public goods", body:"Why doesn't the market provide street lighting?", answer:"It's a <strong>public good</strong>: people can use it without paying, so private businesses can't profit from it."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying South Africa is a pure market economy.","Describing systems without their advantages and disadvantages.","Forgetting the three basic economic questions."],
    callout:{title:"The big debate", body:"How much should the state do — and how much should markets do? South African politics constantly debates this balance."},
    activity:"Make a table comparing market, command and mixed economies: ownership, decision-making, one advantage and one disadvantage.",
    activityAnswer:"Market: private, prices/profit, efficient, inequality. Command: state, central plan, aims at equality, shortages/inefficiency. Mixed: both, prices plus government, balances efficiency and fairness, risk of state inefficiency or excessive regulation.",
    examTip:"For “evaluate” questions on economic systems, give advantages AND disadvantages and use South African examples.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Market economy",d:"Private ownership; prices guide decisions"},{w:"Command economy",d:"State ownership and central planning"},{w:"Mixed economy",d:"Combination of market and government decisions"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"South Africa has a:",opts:["Pure market economy","Command economy","Mixed economy","Traditional economy"],a:2,et:"Private business plus government."},
      {q:"In a command economy, decisions are made by:",opts:["Consumers","The state","Banks","Stokvels"],a:1,et:"Central planning."},
      {q:"Street lights are an example of a:",opts:["Public good","Luxury good","Inferior good","Export"],a:0,et:"Non-excludable, so markets under-provide them."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Every economy answers what, how and for whom. South Africa answers with a mix of markets and government."},

  5: {emoji:"🇿🇦", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · How South Africa earns its living",
    illusText:"Most South Africans who work are in services — shops, offices, transport and government.",
    hook:{title:"A mining country? Not anymore", body:"South Africa built its wealth on gold and diamonds, so many people still think of it as a mining economy. But today mining is under a tenth of GDP, and about two-thirds of the economy is <strong>services</strong>: banking, retail, transport, tourism, telecommunications and government. The jobs of the future are mostly in this sector — and so is much of the competition."},
    intro:"Economic activity is grouped into sectors. The <strong>primary sector</strong> extracts natural resources (mining, agriculture, fishing, forestry). The <strong>secondary sector</strong> processes them (manufacturing, construction, electricity). The <strong>tertiary sector</strong> provides services (trade, finance, transport, communication, government). South Africa's GDP was about <strong>R7 trillion</strong> in 2023, with a population of about <strong>62 million</strong> (Census 2022). The tertiary sector contributes about two-thirds of GDP. Key challenges: high <strong>unemployment</strong>, <strong>inequality</strong>, <strong>slow growth</strong>, electricity and logistics constraints, and a large <strong>informal sector</strong>. Strengths include a sophisticated financial sector, good mineral resources and a well-developed (if strained) infrastructure.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⛏️ Primary", body:"Mining and agriculture are a small share of GDP but crucial for exports and rural jobs."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏭 Secondary", body:"Manufacturing (cars, food processing, chemicals) adds value and creates jobs."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏦 Tertiary", body:"About two-thirds of GDP: finance, retail, transport, telecoms, tourism and government services."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Classify", body:"(a) A citrus farm; (b) a juice factory; (c) a supermarket selling the juice.", answer:"(a) <strong>Primary</strong>; (b) <strong>secondary</strong>; (c) <strong>tertiary</strong>."},
      {title:"GDP per person", body:"GDP ≈ R7 trillion; population ≈ 62 million.", answer:"7 000 000 000 000 ÷ 62 000 000 ≈ <strong>R113 000 per person per year</strong> (an average that hides huge inequality)."},
      {title:"Why services?", body:"Why has the tertiary sector grown?", answer:"<strong>Urbanisation, rising incomes and technology</strong> increase demand for finance, retail, telecoms and other services."},
    ],
    formula:"GDP per capita = GDP ÷ Population",
    mistakes:["Saying South Africa's economy is mainly mining.","Confusing secondary (manufacturing) and tertiary (services).","Treating GDP per capita as what an ordinary person earns."],
    callout:{title:"Informal sector", body:"Spaza shops, street vendors and taxi operators make up a large informal sector that official figures can under-count."},
    activity:"Trace a product (e.g. a loaf of bread) through the primary, secondary and tertiary sectors. Name the activity in each.",
    activityAnswer:"Primary: farming wheat. Secondary: milling flour and baking bread. Tertiary: transporting it and selling it in a shop.",
    examTip:"Use current, rounded statistics with their source and year (e.g. “about 62 million people, Census 2022”).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Primary sector",d:"Extracting natural resources"},{w:"Secondary sector",d:"Manufacturing and construction"},{w:"Tertiary sector",d:"Providing services"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A car factory is in the:",opts:["Primary sector","Secondary sector","Tertiary sector","Informal sector only"],a:1,et:"Manufacturing."},
      {q:"The largest share of South Africa's GDP comes from:",opts:["Mining","Agriculture","Services (tertiary)","Fishing"],a:2,et:"About two-thirds."},
      {q:"South Africa's population (Census 2022) was about:",opts:["42 million","52 million","62 million","72 million"],a:2,et:"About 62 million."},
    ],
    conclusion:"South Africa is a services economy built on mineral wealth. Knowing its structure explains its strengths and its struggles."},
};

// ─── Module 1: Macroeconomics ───────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.economics.m1 = {
  0: {emoji:"📊", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Measuring the size of the economy",
    illusText:"When Stats SA says “the economy grew 0.6%”, it's talking about GDP.",
    hook:{title:"“The economy grew 0.6%”", body:"Every three months, Stats SA announces how much South Africa's economy grew or shrank, and the news reports it as a headline. That number is the change in <strong>Gross Domestic Product (GDP)</strong> — the total value of everything produced in the country. It affects jobs, interest rates and government budgets. But how is it measured, and what does it miss?"},
    intro:"<strong>GDP</strong> is the total value of all <strong>final</strong> goods and services produced <strong>within a country's borders</strong> in a period. Using the <strong>expenditure method</strong>: <strong>GDP = C + G + I + (X − M)</strong> — household consumption, government spending, investment and net exports. <strong>GNI</strong> (Gross National Income) adds income earned by South Africans abroad and subtracts income earned by foreigners here. <strong>Nominal GDP</strong> uses current prices; <strong>real GDP</strong> removes inflation, so it shows true growth. <strong>GDP per capita</strong> = GDP ÷ population. GDP doesn't measure unpaid work, the informal sector fully, inequality or wellbeing.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧾 Expenditure method", body:"Add up spending: consumers (C), government (G), investment (I), exports (X) minus imports (M)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📏 Real vs nominal", body:"If GDP rises 7% but prices rose 5%, real growth is only about 2%."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌍 GDP vs GNI", body:"GDP = produced here. GNI = earned by South African residents, wherever it's produced."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Calculate GDP", body:"C = 4 000, G = 1 400, I = 1 000, X = 2 100, M = 2 000 (R billion).", answer:"4 000 + 1 400 + 1 000 + (2 100 − 2 000) = <strong>R6 500 billion</strong>."},
      {title:"Real growth", body:"Nominal GDP grew 7% and inflation was 5%.", answer:"Real growth ≈ 7 − 5 = <strong>about 2%</strong>."},
      {title:"What GDP misses", body:"Name one thing GDP does not measure.", answer:"<strong>Unpaid work</strong> (caring for children), <strong>inequality</strong>, or environmental damage."},
    ],
    formula:"GDP = C + G + I + (X − M)",
    mistakes:["Counting intermediate goods (double counting).","Forgetting to subtract imports.","Confusing nominal and real GDP."],
    callout:{title:"Beyond GDP", body:"A country can grow GDP while most people's lives don't improve — that's why economists also look at HDI, inequality and unemployment."},
    activity:"C = R3 200 bn, G = R1 100 bn, I = R700 bn, X = R1 500 bn, M = R1 700 bn. Calculate GDP. Is there a trade surplus or deficit?",
    activityAnswer:"3 200 + 1 100 + 700 + (1 500 − 1 700) = <strong>R4 800 bn</strong>. X − M = −200 → a trade <strong>deficit</strong>.",
    examTip:"Write the formula, substitute with units, and comment on the result (e.g. what a negative net export value means).",
    keyterms:[{w:"GDP",d:"Value of final goods and services produced within a country"},{w:"Real GDP",d:"GDP adjusted for inflation"},{w:"GNI",d:"Income earned by a country's residents, at home and abroad"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In GDP = C + G + I + (X − M), M stands for:",opts:["Money","Imports","Manufacturing","Mining"],a:1,et:"Imports are subtracted."},
      {q:"Real GDP is adjusted for:",opts:["Population","Inflation","Exports","Tax"],a:1,et:"It removes price changes."},
      {q:"Which does GDP NOT include?",opts:["Government spending","Unpaid household work","Investment","Exports"],a:1,et:"Unpaid work isn't measured."},
    ],
    conclusion:"GDP measures the size of the economy — useful, but not the whole story of how people live."},

  1: {emoji:"🛒", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Why your money buys less each year",
    illusText:"The same R100 buys less each year. That's inflation.",
    hook:{title:"What R100 buys now", body:"Ask an older relative what a loaf of bread, a taxi trip or a litre of petrol cost ten years ago. Prices almost always go up — so the same R100 buys less each year. That steady rise in the general price level is <strong>inflation</strong>. Stats SA measures it every month, and the Reserve Bank's job is to keep it low and stable."},
    intro:"<strong>Inflation</strong> is a sustained increase in the <strong>general price level</strong>. Stats SA measures it with the <strong>Consumer Price Index (CPI)</strong>, which tracks the prices of a <strong>basket</strong> of goods and services that typical households buy (food, transport, housing, electricity, etc.). <strong>Inflation rate = (CPI this year − CPI last year) ÷ CPI last year × 100</strong>. Causes: <strong>demand-pull</strong> (too much spending chasing too few goods) and <strong>cost-push</strong> (higher costs such as fuel, electricity or wages). Inflation hurts people on fixed incomes and savers, and makes planning harder. The <strong>SARB</strong> targets low inflation (for many years a 3–6% range; from 2025 a lower 3% target — check the current target).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧺 CPI basket", body:"A weighted list of what households buy; heavier weights for items people spend more on."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⛽ Cost-push", body:"Fuel and electricity price rises push up costs across the economy — common in South Africa."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📉 Who loses?", body:"Pensioners, savers and workers whose wages don't rise as fast as prices."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Inflation rate", body:"CPI rose from 110 to 115.5.", answer:"(115.5 − 110) ÷ 110 × 100 = <strong>5%</strong>."},
      {title:"Real wage", body:"Wages rose 4%, inflation was 6%.", answer:"Real wages <strong>fell by about 2%</strong> — workers can buy less."},
      {title:"Type of inflation", body:"Eskom tariffs rise sharply, and businesses raise prices.", answer:"<strong>Cost-push inflation</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"Inflation rate = (CPI new − CPI old) ÷ CPI old × 100",
    mistakes:["Saying inflation means all prices rise equally.","Dividing by the new CPI instead of the old one.","Confusing a high price level with a high inflation rate."],
    callout:{title:"Stokvels and inflation", body:"Money saved under the mattress loses value. Stokvels and savings accounts that earn interest help protect savings from inflation."},
    activity:"A basket cost R1 200 last year and R1 266 this year. Calculate the inflation rate.",
    activityAnswer:"(1 266 − 1 200) ÷ 1 200 × 100 = <strong>5.5%</strong>.",
    examTip:"Show the formula and working, and say who gains and who loses from the inflation you calculated.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Inflation",d:"Sustained rise in the general price level"},{w:"CPI",d:"Consumer Price Index — measures changes in consumer prices"},{w:"Cost-push inflation",d:"Inflation caused by rising production costs"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"CPI rises from 200 to 210. Inflation:",opts:["10%","5%","2%","20%"],a:1,et:"10 ÷ 200 × 100 = 5%."},
      {q:"In South Africa, CPI is published by:",opts:["SARS","Stats SA","Eskom","The JSE"],a:1,et:"Monthly."},
      {q:"Rising fuel prices causing inflation is:",opts:["Demand-pull","Cost-push","Deflation","Recession"],a:1,et:"Higher costs push prices up."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Inflation quietly shrinks what your money can buy. Measuring it is the first step to controlling it."},

  2: {emoji:"🧑‍💼", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · South Africa's biggest economic challenge",
    illusText:"About six in ten young people aged 15–24 who want work can't find it.",
    hook:{title:"The CV in every WhatsApp group", body:"Scroll through any community WhatsApp group and you'll see it: young people sharing CVs and asking about job openings. South Africa has one of the highest <strong>unemployment</strong> rates in the world — around a third of the labour force, and about six in ten young people aged 15–24 who are looking for work. Understanding why is the first step to solving it."},
    intro:"A person is <strong>unemployed</strong> (official, narrow definition) if they are of working age (15–64), without work, available to work and <strong>actively looked for work</strong> recently. The <strong>expanded definition</strong> also includes <strong>discouraged work-seekers</strong> who want work but have stopped looking. Stats SA measures this in the <strong>Quarterly Labour Force Survey (QLFS)</strong>. <strong>Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force × 100</strong> (labour force = employed + unemployed). Types: <strong>structural</strong> (skills don't match available jobs — the biggest in South Africa), <strong>cyclical</strong> (during recessions), <strong>frictional</strong> (between jobs) and <strong>seasonal</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧩 Structural unemployment", body:"Skills mismatch, poor education outcomes, distance from jobs (apartheid spatial planning) and slow growth."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📉 Cyclical unemployment", body:"Jobs lost when the economy shrinks — e.g. during COVID-19 lockdowns."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🛠️ Solutions", body:"Faster growth, better education and skills, support for small businesses, public employment programmes and fixing electricity and logistics."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Calculate", body:"7.9 million unemployed; 16.8 million employed.", answer:"7.9 ÷ (7.9 + 16.8) × 100 = 7.9 ÷ 24.7 × 100 ≈ <strong>32.0%</strong>."},
      {title:"Classify", body:"A mineworker loses his job when a mine closes because of new machines.", answer:"<strong>Structural</strong> unemployment (technological change)."},
      {title:"Discouraged", body:"Thabo wants a job but stopped looking after a year of rejections.", answer:"He is a <strong>discouraged work-seeker</strong> — counted only in the expanded definition."},
    ],
    formula:"Unemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ (Employed + Unemployed) × 100",
    mistakes:["Dividing by the whole population instead of the labour force.","Confusing the official and expanded definitions.","Saying all unemployment is cyclical."],
    callout:{title:"Youth", body:"Matric, further study and practical skills sharply improve job chances. Programmes like learnerships and youth employment initiatives target this group."},
    activity:"In a town, 18 000 people are employed, 6 000 are unemployed and actively looking, and 2 000 are discouraged. Calculate the official and expanded unemployment rates.",
    activityAnswer:"Official: 6 000 ÷ 24 000 × 100 = <strong>25%</strong>. Expanded: 8 000 ÷ 26 000 × 100 ≈ <strong>30.8%</strong>.",
    examTip:"State which definition you're using, show the formula, and link the type of unemployment to a suitable solution.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Labour force",d:"Employed plus unemployed people"},{w:"Structural unemployment",d:"Mismatch between workers' skills and available jobs"},{w:"Discouraged work-seeker",d:"Wants work but has stopped looking"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"South Africa's unemployment statistics come from the:",opts:["QLFS by Stats SA","SARB","JSE","Census only"],a:0,et:"Quarterly Labour Force Survey."},
      {q:"The biggest type of unemployment in SA is:",opts:["Seasonal","Frictional","Structural","None"],a:2,et:"Skills mismatch and slow growth."},
      {q:"6 unemployed, 24 employed. Rate:",opts:["20%","25%","30%","6%"],a:0,et:"6 ÷ 30 × 100 = 20%."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Unemployment is South Africa's defining challenge. Measure it correctly, understand its type, match the solution."},

  3: {emoji:"🎢", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Booms, recessions and recoveries",
    illusText:"In 2020, COVID-19 lockdowns caused one of the sharpest falls in South Africa's GDP in decades.",
    hook:{title:"2020: the economy hits the brakes", body:"In 2020, lockdowns closed shops, taxis, factories and schools. South Africa's GDP fell sharply — one of the biggest annual drops in decades — and over a million jobs were lost. Then, slowly, it recovered. Economies always move in waves of growth and decline. This pattern is called the <strong>business cycle</strong>."},
    intro:"The <strong>business cycle</strong> describes fluctuations in real GDP over time. Phases: <strong>expansion/upswing</strong> (rising output, jobs and spending), <strong>peak/boom</strong> (highest point, inflation pressure), <strong>contraction/downswing</strong> (falling output and jobs) and <strong>trough</strong> (lowest point) before <strong>recovery</strong>. A <strong>recession</strong> is often defined as <strong>two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth</strong>. The SARB uses <strong>leading indicators</strong> (which change before the economy does, e.g. building plans passed, business confidence) to predict turns. Causes of fluctuations include changes in spending, investment, interest rates, global events and shocks such as droughts, load shedding or pandemics.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📈 Upswing", body:"More spending → more production → more jobs → more income → more spending."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📉 Downswing", body:"Less spending → lower production → job losses → less income → even less spending."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔭 Indicators", body:"Leading (predict), coincident (move with) and lagging (follow) indicators help forecast the cycle."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Recession?", body:"Real GDP growth: Q1 −0.2%, Q2 −0.4%.", answer:"Two consecutive negative quarters → <strong>technical recession</strong>."},
      {title:"Phase", body:"Unemployment is falling and factories are hiring.", answer:"<strong>Expansion/upswing</strong>."},
      {title:"Leading indicator", body:"Why are building plans passed a leading indicator?", answer:"They show <strong>future construction activity</strong> before it happens, so they predict economic direction."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Labelling the phases in the wrong order.","Saying one negative quarter is a recession.","Forgetting that the cycle is measured with real GDP."],
    callout:{title:"Policy response", body:"In a downswing, government and the SARB can try to boost spending (lower interest rates, more government spending); in a boom, they try to cool inflation."},
    activity:"Draw and label a business cycle graph showing peak, trough, upswing, downswing and the trend line.",
    activityAnswer:"A wave above and below a rising trend line: label the top as peak, the bottom as trough, the rising part as upswing/expansion and the falling part as downswing/contraction; the x-axis is time, the y-axis real GDP.",
    examTip:"Business-cycle graphs earn marks for correct labels: axes, phases, trend line, amplitude and length of the cycle.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Recession",d:"Two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth"},{w:"Trough",d:"The lowest point of the business cycle"},{w:"Leading indicator",d:"A measure that changes before the economy does"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A technical recession is:",opts:["One quarter of growth","Two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth","High inflation","A trade deficit"],a:1,et:"Two quarters in a row."},
      {q:"The highest point of the business cycle is the:",opts:["Trough","Peak","Recovery","Trend"],a:1,et:"Then a downswing follows."},
      {q:"Rising job numbers suggest:",opts:["A downswing","An upswing","A trough","Deflation"],a:1,et:"Expansion."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Economies rise and fall in cycles. Knowing the phases helps you understand the news — and the policies that respond."},

  4: {emoji:"🏦", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Government budgets and interest rates",
    illusText:"In February the Minister of Finance presents the Budget; the SARB sets interest rates through the year.",
    hook:{title:"Budget day and repo-rate day", body:"Every February, the Minister of Finance stands in Parliament and announces the <strong>Budget</strong>: how much government will spend on grants, schools and hospitals, and which taxes will change. Several times a year, the Reserve Bank announces whether the <strong>repo rate</strong> will go up, down or stay the same. Both decisions affect your family's money — that's <strong>fiscal</strong> and <strong>monetary policy</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Fiscal policy</strong> is government's use of <strong>spending and taxation</strong> to influence the economy, run by <strong>National Treasury</strong> through the annual Budget. <strong>Expansionary</strong> fiscal policy (more spending, lower taxes) boosts demand in a downswing; <strong>contractionary</strong> policy (less spending, higher taxes) cools the economy and reduces debt. Taxes include personal income tax, company tax, and <strong>VAT</strong> (15%). <strong>Monetary policy</strong> is the <strong>SARB</strong>'s use of the <strong>repo rate</strong> (the rate at which it lends to banks) to control inflation. Banks' <strong>prime lending rate</strong> is usually the repo rate plus 3.5 percentage points. Raising rates makes borrowing more expensive, reduces spending and lowers inflation; cutting rates does the opposite.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🏛️ Fiscal policy", body:"Treasury: spending (grants, infrastructure, education) and taxes (income tax, VAT, fuel levy)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"💱 Monetary policy", body:"SARB: repo rate and money supply, aimed mainly at keeping inflation low and stable."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏠 Effect on families", body:"Higher interest rates → higher home-loan, car and credit-card repayments; higher returns on savings."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Prime rate", body:"The repo rate is 7%.", answer:"Prime ≈ 7 + 3.5 = <strong>10.5%</strong>."},
      {title:"Rate rise", body:"Interest on a R50 000 loan at 10.5% vs 10.75% per year.", answer:"R5 250 vs R5 375 → <strong>R125 more per year</strong>."},
      {title:"VAT", body:"An item costs R200 before VAT at 15%.", answer:"VAT = 200 × 0.15 = <strong>R30</strong>; price = R230."},
    ],
    formula:"Prime rate ≈ Repo rate + 3.5 percentage points",
    mistakes:["Saying the SARB sets taxes — Treasury does (through Parliament).","Mixing up expansionary and contractionary policy.","Thinking higher interest rates increase spending."],
    callout:{title:"Read the news", body:"When the SARB's Monetary Policy Committee announces a decision, check how it changes prime — and what that means for a car or home loan."},
    activity:"The economy is in a downswing with low inflation. Recommend one fiscal and one monetary policy action, and explain each.",
    activityAnswer:"Fiscal: increase infrastructure spending to create jobs and demand. Monetary: cut the repo rate so borrowing is cheaper and spending rises.",
    examTip:"Always say who runs the policy (Treasury or SARB), what tool is used, and its effect on spending, output and inflation.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Fiscal policy",d:"Government spending and taxation decisions"},{w:"Monetary policy",d:"SARB decisions on interest rates and money supply"},{w:"Repo rate",d:"Rate at which the SARB lends to banks"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Fiscal policy is run by:",opts:["The SARB","National Treasury","Stats SA","The JSE"],a:1,et:"Through the Budget."},
      {q:"To reduce inflation, the SARB would usually:",opts:["Cut the repo rate","Raise the repo rate","Print more money","Cut VAT"],a:1,et:"Higher rates reduce spending."},
      {q:"Repo rate 8% → prime is about:",opts:["8%","9.5%","11.5%","15%"],a:2,et:"8 + 3.5."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Treasury taxes and spends; the SARB sets interest rates. Together they steer the economy."},

  5: {emoji:"🏛️", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Protecting the value of the rand",
    illusText:"Founded in 1921, the SARB is one of the oldest central banks in Africa.",
    hook:{title:"The bank that doesn't take your deposit", body:"You can't open an account at the <strong>South African Reserve Bank (SARB)</strong>, but its decisions affect every rand you earn and spend. Founded in <strong>1921</strong>, it issues our banknotes and coins and sets the repo rate. Its Governor, Lesetja Kganyago (in office since 2014), announces interest-rate decisions that make headlines across the country."},
    intro:"The <strong>SARB</strong> is South Africa's <strong>central bank</strong>. Its independence is protected by <strong>Section 224 of the Constitution</strong>, and its main mandate is to <strong>protect the value of the currency</strong> in the interest of balanced and sustainable growth — mainly by keeping <strong>inflation</strong> low and stable. Functions: conducting <strong>monetary policy</strong> (the <strong>Monetary Policy Committee</strong> meets six times a year to set the repo rate), <strong>issuing banknotes and coins</strong>, acting as <strong>banker to the government</strong> and <strong>banker to the banks</strong> (lender of last resort), managing the country's <strong>gold and foreign-exchange reserves</strong>, and supervising banks to keep the financial system stable (through the Prudential Authority).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🎯 Mandate", body:"Price stability (low, stable inflation) — so money keeps its value."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏦 Banker's bank", body:"Commercial banks hold accounts at the SARB and can borrow from it at the repo rate."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🛡️ Independence", body:"Protected by the Constitution so that rate decisions aren't driven by short-term politics."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Function", body:"Who issues South Africa's banknotes?", answer:"The <strong>SARB</strong>."},
      {title:"Independence", body:"Why should a central bank be independent?", answer:"So it can make <strong>unpopular but necessary decisions</strong> (like raising rates) to control inflation without political pressure."},
      {title:"Lender of last resort", body:"What does it mean that the SARB is a lender of last resort?", answer:"It can <strong>lend to banks in trouble</strong> to prevent a bank collapse from spreading through the financial system."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Confusing the SARB with commercial banks.","Saying the SARB decides government spending.","Forgetting the constitutional protection of its independence."],
    callout:{title:"Your banknotes", body:"The SARB designs banknotes with security features — watermarks, security threads and colour-shifting ink — to stop counterfeiting."},
    activity:"List five functions of the SARB and give a one-line example of each.",
    activityAnswer:"Monetary policy (sets the repo rate); issues notes and coins; banker to government (holds its accounts); banker to banks (lender of last resort); manages reserves (gold and foreign currency); supervises banks (Prudential Authority).",
    examTip:"Learn the SARB's functions as a list and be ready to explain how changing the repo rate affects inflation step by step.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Central bank",d:"A country's main monetary authority"},{w:"MPC",d:"Monetary Policy Committee — sets the repo rate"},{w:"Lender of last resort",d:"Lends to banks in a crisis to protect the system"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The SARB was founded in:",opts:["1910","1921","1961","1994"],a:1,et:"One of Africa's oldest central banks."},
      {q:"The SARB's independence is protected by:",opts:["The Companies Act","The Constitution","The LRA","The Freedom Charter"],a:1,et:"Section 224."},
      {q:"The SARB's MPC meets about:",opts:["Once a year","Six times a year","Every day","Every ten years"],a:1,et:"To decide the repo rate."},
    ],
    conclusion:"The SARB guards the value of the rand. Its independence and its tools — especially the repo rate — matter to every household."},
};

